  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reducing deforestation in community-owned lands in Amazonas systems, and payments for ecosystems services schemes.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Amazonas Region in Peru is world-renowned for its high levels of biodiversity and endemism, as well as high levels of deforestation driven by unsustainable livestock and agriculture practices. Protected areas in the region are not sufficiently large or connected to protect biodiversity making private and community-based conservation initiatives essential. In Amazonas, there are currently 14 officially recognized Privately Protected Areas (PPAs) led by local individuals or communities that together protect 264,829 acres and many endangered species yellow-tailed woolly monkey, the long-whiskered owlet, the spatula tail hummingbird, and the Andean night monkey. The PPAs formed the Amazonas Network of Private Conservation and is now beginning its first joint projects. This project will work with two community-based PPAs that have particular ecological importance and are under acute threat due to deforestation but also have ideal conditions to apply proven approaches to address deforestation caused by unsustainable land-use practices. Specific activities include improving and strengthening two PPAs through participatory mapping to establish zoning borders and rules, implementing silvopastoral techniques that integrate trees with forage and livestock production, and solidifying and formalizing a payment for ecosystem services scheme between communities that will incentivize forest protection.</narrative>
  </description>
